---
// src/pages/en/projects/circon.astro
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro"; // Adjust path if your BaseLayout is elsewhere

// --- Frontmatter for the Project Page ---
const frontmatter = {
  title: "Project Circon - Advanced Data Visualization Platform",
  navTitle: "Project Circon", // Optional: Shorter title for navigation
  description:
    "An in-depth look at Project Circon, a platform designed for real-time data analysis and visualization, helping businesses make informed decisions.",
  publishDate: "2024-05-08",
  heroImage: "/images/projects/circon-hero.jpg", // Example path, replace with your actual image
  tags: [
    "Data Visualization",
    "Real-time Analytics",
    "SaaS",
    "Platform Development",
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout title={frontmatter.title}>
  <article class="project-detail-page prose dark:prose-invert prose-lg mx-auto">
    {
      frontmatter.heroImage && (
        <div class="hero-image-container mb-8">
          <img
            src={frontmatter.heroImage}
            alt={`${frontmatter.title} hero image`}
            class="w-full h-auto rounded-lg shadow-lg object-cover max-h-96"
          />
        </div>
      )
    }

    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mb-4 text-center md:text-left">
      {frontmatter.title}
    </h1>

    <div
      class="project-meta text-center md:text-left mb-8 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400"
    >
      {
        frontmatter.publishDate && (
          <time datetime={frontmatter.publishDate}>
            Published:{" "}
            {new Date(frontmatter.publishDate).toLocaleDateString("en-US", {
              year: "numeric",
              month: "long",
              day: "numeric",
            })}
          </time>
        )
      }
      {
        frontmatter.tags && frontmatter.tags.length > 0 && (
          <div class="tags mt-2">
            <strong>Tags:</strong>
            {frontmatter.tags.map((tag) => (
              <span class="tag inline-block bg-slate-200 dark:bg-slate-700 rounded-full px-3 py-1 text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-200 mr-2 mb-2">
                #{tag}
              </span>
            ))}
          </div>
        )
      }
    </div>

    <section class="content-section mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Project Overview</h2>
      <p>
        Project Circon is a cutting-edge platform developed to address the
        growing need for intuitive and powerful real-time data visualization and
        analytics. In an era where data is king, Circon empowers businesses to
        unlock insights from complex datasets through an accessible and highly
        customizable interface.
      </p>
      <p>
        The core mission was to build a scalable, performant, and user-friendly
        tool that could integrate seamlessly with various data sources and
        provide actionable intelligence at a glance.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Key Features</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2">
        <li>
          <strong>Real-time Dashboarding:</strong> Dynamic dashboards that update
          instantaneously with incoming data streams.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Interactive Charts & Graphs:</strong> A rich library of customizable
          charts (bar, line, pie, scatter, heatmaps, etc.) with drill-down capabilities.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Multi-Source Data Integration:</strong> Connectors for popular
          databases, APIs, and cloud services.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Advanced Filtering & Segmentation:</strong> Tools to easily slice
          and dice data for granular analysis.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Alerting & Notifications:</strong> Customizable alerts based on
          predefined thresholds or anomalous patterns.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Collaborative Workspaces:</strong> Share dashboards and insights
          with team members securely.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Responsive Design:</strong> Fully accessible on desktops, tablets,
          and mobile devices.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Technologies Used</h2>
      <p>
        The platform was built using a modern technology stack, focusing on
        performance, scalability, and developer experience:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
        <li>
          <strong>Frontend:</strong> React, TypeScript, D3.js (for custom visualizations),
          Tailwind CSS
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Backend:</strong> Node.js with Express.js, Python (for data processing
          microservices)
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Database:</strong> PostgreSQL, Redis (for caching and session management)
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Infrastructure:</strong> Docker, Kubernetes, AWS (EC2, S3, RDS)
        </li>
        <li><strong>Real-time Communication:</strong> WebSockets</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Challenges & Solutions</h2>
      <p>
        One of the primary challenges was handling high-velocity data streams
        and rendering complex visualizations without performance degradation.
        This was addressed by implementing efficient data aggregation techniques
        on the backend, optimizing frontend rendering with virtualized lists and
        memoization, and leveraging WebAssembly for computationally intensive
        tasks.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ensuring data security and compliance was another critical aspect, met
        by robust authentication mechanisms, role-based access control, and
        end-to-end encryption.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Project Outcome</h2>
      <p>
        Project Circon successfully launched and has been adopted by several
        pilot companies, receiving positive feedback for its performance and
        ease of use. It has enabled users to gain deeper insights into their
        operations, leading to improved efficiency and data-driven
        decision-making.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Scoped styles for the project page to ensure Raleway font is used. */
  /* The global.css already sets --font-sans to Raleway for the html element. */
  /* This rule ensures that the main article content specifically uses it, */
  /* overriding any potential default font stacks from Tailwind's prose if necessary. */
  .project-detail-page {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
  }

  /* Example: specific aspect ratio or other styles for the hero image container */
  .hero-image-container {
    /* Add any custom styles for the hero image container if needed */
  }

  /* You can further customize heading fonts within the project page if desired, */
  /* though they should inherit from --font-sans by default. */
  /* For example:
  .project-detail-page h1,
  .project-detail-page h2,
  .project-detail-page h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-sans); // Explicitly set if needed
    font-weight: 800; // Or your desired weight for headings
  }
  */
</style>
